# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:home.spec.ts::home.spec.ts_>_home_page_>_shows_the_store_name_as_its_heading

## Diagnosis

The only production file in the declared edit root is `services/web/server.js`. It serves a single hard-coded HTML document for every request. Its top-level heading is a generic greeting:

```
<h1>Welcome</h1>
```

while the page title already names the store (`<title>Corner Shop</title>`). The spec (`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` plus the suite under `tests/`) treats the home page's heading as the place where the store's name belongs. The Playwright suite runs `tests/playwright.config.ts`, which boots this exact file as its `webServer` (`node ../services/web/server.js`) before any test, so the page the test reads is produced solely by `services/web/server.js`. The failing assertion reads `h1` once (no retry) and compares it to `"Corner Shop"`, receiving `"Welcome"`. This is a production-source defect, not a test defect.

## Contract to satisfy

The document served at `GET /` on the configured port must:

1. Contain exactly one `<h1>` element whose text content is precisely `Corner Shop` — no surrounding whitespace, no nested markup, no extra words, matching byte-for-byte under `toBe`. Playwright's `textContent()` returns the raw node text, so do not introduce newlines or indentation inside the heading element.
2. Continue to contain exactly one `<a>` element whose `href` attribute is exactly `/hours` — the first (and only) anchor matched by the `a` locator. Do not add further anchors (e.g. a nav link or footer link), because a second `<a>` would make the `a` locator ambiguous and break the currently passing sibling test. Likewise do not add a second `<h1>`.
3. Keep serving the response synchronously with HTTP 200 and `Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8`, and keep listening on `Number(process.env.PORT)` at `127.0.0.1`, since `playwright.config.ts` supplies `PORT` and polls `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/` as the readiness URL. Any change to the port source, host binding, status code, or startup timing would stall or fail `webServer` startup and take the whole suite down.

## Change to make

**File: `services/web/server.js` (the only file to touch; no new files are needed).**

Replace the heading text in the `page` template literal so the top-level heading carries the store's name — `Corner Shop` — instead of `Welcome`. Keep the element a single-line `<h1>Corner Shop</h1>` inside the existing `<body>`, keeping the anchor, the `<title>`, the doctype, and the response/listen logic exactly as they are.

If the writer prefers to avoid duplicating the literal string in both `<title>` and `<h1>`, it is acceptable to introduce a single module-level constant in the same file (e.g. `const storeName = 'Corner Shop';`) and interpolate it into both places — provided the rendered heading text remains exactly `Corner Shop` with no added whitespace inside the element. This is optional; the minimal literal edit is sufficient and lower risk.

Also update the stale comment block at the top of `services/web/server.js`, which currently describes the heading as "the planted defect" saying `Welcome`. After the repair that comment is false; reword it to describe what the file actually does (serves the Corner Shop home page with the store name as its heading and a link to the opening hours).

